WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.638
- All right, so today

00:00:01.638 --> 00:00:03.993
we are talking
about rotations.

00:00:03.993 --> 00:00:05.880
Now, hold on
to the table, everybody.

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:07.880
We're rotating right now.

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:09.840
Oh, did you know this?
We're rotating.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:11.412
- Yeah.
What do you mean?

00:00:11.412 --> 00:00:12.944
- We've got the Earth
right here.

00:00:12.944 --> 00:00:14.477
The Earth is rotating,

00:00:14.477 --> 00:00:16.048
and we are on the Earth.

00:00:16.048 --> 00:00:18.914
And Cassidy is amazing.
She's hooked up the Earth

00:00:18.914 --> 00:00:21.080
to that plate of...
Oh, what are they?

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:22.600
- Danishes or something?
- Oh, wow.

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:24.640
They look tasty.
Apple cakes?

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:26.513
Oh, look at that.
- Look good.

00:00:26.513 --> 00:00:28.863
- So, yes, we are
rotating right now.

00:00:28.863 --> 00:00:31.316
And, well, that's just
the Earth spinning.

00:00:31.316 --> 00:00:32.840
You were talking about this.

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:34.720
- Well, then the Earth is
going around the sun.

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:37.400
The sun's going, you know,
around in the galaxy.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:39.791
The galaxy is spinning around
in the cosmos somewhere,

00:00:39.791 --> 00:00:42.508
I don't know.
- Wow, everything is spinning.

00:00:42.508 --> 00:00:45.200
- Why not?
- It's just a part of life.

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:47.722
And if you go all the way back
 to the Big Bang,

00:00:47.722 --> 00:00:49.828
when particles were created,

00:00:49.828 --> 00:00:51.655
there were little fluctuations,

00:00:51.655 --> 00:00:53.546
quantum fluctuations,
things, like,

00:00:53.546 --> 00:00:55.552
gravitationally attracted
to each other,

00:00:55.552 --> 00:00:57.080
and just started spinning.

00:00:57.080 --> 00:00:59.611
And we've been spinning
ever since.

00:00:59.611 --> 00:01:01.386
And I love looking
at the Earth right now

00:01:01.386 --> 00:01:03.230
because we're
filming this, like,

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:06.316
a week since
the Artemis II mission

00:01:06.316 --> 00:01:08.046
successfully came back to Earth,
and they have

00:01:08.046 --> 00:01:09.960
beautiful photos.

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:12.400
- It was a moon or an Earthrise.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:14.760
They had it rise, really
nice one over the surface of it.

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:16.280
- My youngest was like,
"Is that AI?"

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:18.557
And I'm like,
no, that is proper.

00:01:18.557 --> 00:01:20.785
So I thought, the Earth
is a good, like,

00:01:20.785 --> 00:01:24.440
kind of demonstration
of rotation.

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:25.960
The Earth does rotate.

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:29.848
It rotates 360 degrees
in one day,

00:01:29.848 --> 00:01:31.899
and it doesn't rotate about.

00:01:31.899 --> 00:01:33.896
You see, this Earth
has a certain

00:01:33.896 --> 00:01:36.422
angle for the axis
of rotation,

00:01:36.422 --> 00:01:39.596
and the angle is with
respect to the plane.

00:01:39.596 --> 00:01:41.467
Like Scott was saying,
the Earth is going

00:01:41.467 --> 00:01:43.913
around the sun
once a year.

00:01:43.913 --> 00:01:45.423
So yeah, it's a little bit,
I think,

00:01:45.423 --> 00:01:48.680
20 degrees off the vertical.
- Something like that.

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:52.471
- 23.5
- Oh, 23.5! Thank you.

00:01:52.471 --> 00:01:54.000
Well, it's an important number.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.207
Why is it important,
23.5 degrees,

00:01:56.207 --> 00:01:58.040
the axis of rotation?

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:00.218
- So we can have seasons.

00:02:00.218 --> 00:02:01.816
As you go around,

00:02:01.816 --> 00:02:04.080
we tilt a little bit more,
 and the sun doesn't go as high.

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:06.732
Then in the summertime,
 the sun goes higher,

00:02:06.732 --> 00:02:09.152
especially here
in Canada, you know.

00:02:09.152 --> 00:02:11.560
- And Paul, but just
before we started taping.

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:13.247
You were mentioning how, like,

00:02:13.247 --> 00:02:15.760
the rotation
axis of the Earth

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:17.576
kind of all started
when a big,

00:02:17.576 --> 00:02:19.840
massive, planet-sized object

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:21.973
smashed into us billions
of years ago.

00:02:21.973 --> 00:02:23.600
- Made our moon, right?
- Yeah.

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:25.119
- Carved out a big chunk.

00:02:25.119 --> 00:02:26.960
And they're saying now they
think there's a big lump of it

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:28.983
still kind of inside,
and that could be why we...

00:02:28.983 --> 00:02:30.520
What do you
acually you call it?

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:32.120
- Process.
- Process?

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:33.658
- So we wobble
because of that?

00:02:33.658 --> 00:02:36.037
- Wobble, it could be
(indiscernible)

00:02:36.037 --> 00:02:37.694
Big huge chunk of

00:02:37.694 --> 00:02:39.680
some other planet or whatever.

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:41.800
- Wow.
- In our planet.

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:44.440
- Gross.
- Yeah, gross.

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:46.694
- Sounds like Alien.
- Yeah, exactly.

00:02:46.694 --> 00:02:48.960
You know?
Yeah, it's pretty cool.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:50.886
- So, yeah, I guess
the Earth has

00:02:50.886 --> 00:02:52.840
an axis of rotation,

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:55.470
and it has
an angle of rotation.

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:58.080
Those are the two things
you need to rotate.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:02:59.810
But usually, when you

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:02.320
speak to, like,
regular people,

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:03.958
they kind of think of, like,

00:03:03.958 --> 00:03:05.640
yaw, pitch, and roll.

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:07.410
I remember your brother.

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:09.520
- Yeah, he's a pilot,
 and he likes the

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:12.760
yaw, pitch, and roll
approach to rotations.

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:15.168
So the pitch would be

00:03:15.168 --> 00:03:17.320
the up and down motion
of the pointing which way

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:18.880
the planes' going up and down.

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:21.680
Yaw would be turning left
and right, so to speak.

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:23.600
And then the roll,
he hopes he doesn't do,

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:25.203
but that would...

00:03:25.203 --> 00:03:26.804
- Depending on the kind
of plane you're flying, I guess.

00:03:26.804 --> 00:03:29.264
- He's calm that way.

00:03:29.264 --> 00:03:30.560
(laughing)

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:32.880
And I keep asking, why do we
have to do it in that dumb way?

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:34.520
Why don't we use quaternions?

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:36.560
- Oh, quaternion, my favourite.
We will get there.

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:38.679
And then he says, I don't know.
Go ask Umer.

00:03:38.679 --> 00:03:40.791
- Paul, you could speak about

00:03:40.791 --> 00:03:42.514
these three,
yaw, pitch, and roll,

00:03:42.514 --> 00:03:44.440
because you were
mentioning that animators,

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:46.149
artists do use that

00:03:46.149 --> 00:03:48.948
to make their cool animations.

00:03:48.948 --> 00:03:51.646
Yes, yeah.
But we don't call it,

00:03:51.646 --> 00:03:54.567
you know those three,
we call it just X, Y, and Z.

00:03:54.567 --> 00:03:57.258
Because they, when you kind
 of look at it practically,

00:03:57.258 --> 00:03:59.425
when you think of those three,
 you need to be

00:03:59.425 --> 00:04:01.840
moving in a direction,
necessarily.

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:03.600
You need to have a front
of something to say,

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:06.600
I'm pitching up and down,
as to which is which.

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:09.526
So we're just X, Y, Z
when we look at an object.

00:04:09.526 --> 00:04:11.574
- Wouldn't X, Y, Z
be just as arbitrary.

00:04:11.574 --> 00:04:13.120
- It's just as arbitrary.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:15.000
Yeah, it's absolutely
just as arbitrary.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:16.862
It depends on how you
set up a scene,

00:04:16.862 --> 00:04:18.734
how you set up
a character rig,

00:04:18.734 --> 00:04:20.438
how whatever
you're dealing with...

00:04:20.438 --> 00:04:22.691
- (indiscernible)
The Z goes up thing again.

00:04:22.691 --> 00:04:24.360
- Again, it's arbitrary,
isn't it?

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:25.942
(laughing)

00:04:25.942 --> 00:04:28.251
It matters when it
comes to characters

00:04:28.251 --> 00:04:30.978
animating and stuff
because of the problems

00:04:30.978 --> 00:04:33.245
with Euler angles,
which is gimbal lock,

00:04:33.245 --> 00:04:35.395
which we'll be able
to talk about.

00:04:35.395 --> 00:04:38.400
But, you know, we set up
a character very specifically

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:41.118
so that we try to minimize

00:04:41.118 --> 00:04:43.590
the problems of
an X, Y, Z rotation,

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:45.160
which is we're going
to get into, right?

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:46.734
It's things that
we can do to

00:04:46.734 --> 00:04:48.480
stop the problems
from happening.

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:50.196
Can't stop them,
but you can

00:04:50.196 --> 00:04:52.767
minimize the problems
because

00:04:52.767 --> 00:04:55.621
your quaternions
are the better solution.

00:04:55.621 --> 00:04:57.312
But not for animators.

00:04:57.312 --> 00:04:59.520
- Not as intuitive.

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:01.760
- Yeah, for an artist,
we want curves.

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:03.530
We want to be able to
control how we

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:05.460
get in and out of

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:07.000
a given rotation.

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:09.442
And quaternions don't allow
the same kind of

00:05:09.442 --> 00:05:11.960
flexibility for an artist.

00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:14.522
They just don't
cause problems.

00:05:14.522 --> 00:05:16.080
- Well, I'd love to
see these problems.

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:18.348
And also, like,
how an animator,

00:05:18.348 --> 00:05:20.040
like, animates something.

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:22.264
So maybe we'll take
a quick break,

00:05:22.264 --> 00:05:24.401
and then we can see

00:05:24.401 --> 00:05:26.840
these Euler angles in action.
- Sounds good.